  "modified": "2026-08-12T12:25:34Z",
  "summary": "Next.js router/proxy app with broad child_process + HTTP surface; no traced installer-harm mechanism",
  "details": "Package ships a Next.js server application (LLM router / proxy dashboard) with API routes covering OAuth import from local coding-CLI configs (Codex, Cursor, Cline, Devin, Droid, Grok, Hermes, Kilo, DeepSeek, OpenCode, etc.), provider testing, tunnel management (Tailscale enable/disable/install), CLI settings management, and OpenAI-compatible chat/embeddings/images/videos endpoints. Static patterns match on keyword co-occurrence of require(\"child_process\"), POST/GET, os.homedir(), process.env, hostname, and ping/curl across many minified per-route bundles produced by the Next.js build. This shape is intrinsic to the app's stated purpose: provider validation routes ping and curl user-configured LLM endpoints; CLI-settings routes shell out to read local CLI config files the user has already installed; tunnel routes shell out to tailscale; OpenAI-compatible endpoints forward the caller's requests to the caller-configured provider. cli.js is a launcher: https.get to https://registry.npmjs.org is a version check against the official npm registry, and process.platform branches select platform-specific binaries/paths for the local server. No hardcoded non-first-party exfiltration destination is named in the evidence; no installer-secret path (~/.aws, ~/.ssh, ~/.npmrc, browser stores) is read; no obfuscated destination reconstruction; no lifecycle-hook auto-execution of remote content. The one Buffer.from(...,\"base64\") in oauth/services/xai.js is standard OAuth JWT/PKCE handling. Concerns worth human review are the very broad surface, the mitm/server.js component, and lack of traced-code corroboration for what those routes forward when invoked.",
